Description: network analysis and interception workbench with a Qt interface
 NetLab is a GUI-first network tool for Kali Linux, built around the
 workflow of Intercepter-NG: capture, decode, extract credentials, and --
 when an engagement authorises it -- stand in the middle of the path.
 .
 It has two halves, and the split is deliberate.
 .
 The passive half captures with dumpcap (libpcap) into a real pcap/pcapng
 file that any other tool can read, then decodes those bytes itself:
 .
  * live packet list with decoded detail and hex view
  * connection correlation with per-direction counters and TCP reassembly
  * host and device discovery, topology, and per-device monitoring
  * DNS, mDNS and LLMNR extraction; cleartext HTTP; TLS handshake metadata
  * credential and hash extraction from FTP, Telnet, POP3, IMAP, SMTP,
    LDAP, SNMP, MSSQL, MySQL, PostgreSQL, VNC, Kerberos, HTTP Basic and
    Digest, and NTLMSSP wherever it is carried, in the format hashcat and
    john expect
  * capture file management, import, export and BPF capture filters
 .
 The active half transmits: full-duplex ARP poisoning, SSL stripping, TLS
 interception with a locally generated CA, DNS spoofing, a rogue DHCP
 server, an in-flight traffic changer, cookie expiry, file carving and a
 promiscuous-host probe.
 .
 None of it runs without an engagement: a named, on-disk record of the
 interface, the gateway and the hosts the operator declared in scope,
 confirmed in a dialog that states what will be transmitted. Every address
 is checked against that scope, every action is appended to a JSONL audit
 log, and ARP tables, packet forwarding and firewall rules are restored on
 disarm, on exit and on crash.
 .
 Privileged work runs in netlab-helper, a small separate process started
 through pkexec. The GUI itself stays unprivileged, and when it exits the
 helper restores the network.
